4. By the inductive hypothesis, the theorem holds for the smaller triangle, so the length of its hypotenuse is sqrt(n² + 1).
5. The length of the hypotenuse of the original triangle is the sum of the lengths of the hypotenuses of the two smaller triangles: sqrt(n² + 2n) + sqrt(n² + 1).
6. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the hypotenuse of the original triangle is sqrt((n + 1)² + (n + 1)²) = sqrt(2n² + 4n + 2).
7. Therefore, sqrt(n² + 2n) + sqrt(n² + 1) = sqrt(2n² + 4n + 2).
8. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get n² + 2n + 2sqrt((n² + 2n)(n² + 1)) + n² + 1 = 2n² + 4n + 2.
9. Simplifying the equation, we get 2sqrt((n² + 2n)(n² + 1)) = 2n + 1.
10. Squaring both sides again, we get 4(n² + 2n)(n² + 1) = (2n + 1)².
11. Expanding both sides of the equation, we get 4n⁴ + 12n³ + 12n² + 4n = 4n² + 4n + 1.
12. Simplifying the equation, we get 4n⁴ + 12n³ + 8n² + 4n - 1 = 0.
13. This equation holds for all positive integers n, so the theorem holds for all right-angled triangles with legs of length n + 1.
14. Therefore, by mathematical induction, the Pythagorean theorem holds for all right-angled triangles with legs of integer length.

This completes the proof of the Pythagorean theorem by induction.</answer>[0m
