7. The pigeonhole principle states that if we have more items (numbers) than pigeonholes (unique pairs of primes), then at least one pigeonhole must contain more than one item.
8. In our case, there are infinitely many numbers, but only finitely many unique pairs of primes. Therefore, there must be at least one number that can be expressed as a product of more than one unique pair of primes.
